                0.719,
                0.244
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 10.3: Role of air in the preparation of starch by plants"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.12,
                0.248,
                0.879,
                0.466
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_table_6.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td rowspan=\"2\">Part of the leaf</td><td colspan=\"4\">Availability of</td><td rowspan=\"2\">Starch present (Yes/No)</td></tr><tr><td>Water</td><td>Sunlight</td><td>Chlorophyll</td><td>Carbon dioxide</td></tr><tr><td>Part of the leaf inside the bottle</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Part of the leaf outside the bottle</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.473,
            0.883,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "notice",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that the part of the leaf that was outside the bottle turns blue-black, indicating the presence of starch. However, the part of the leaf inside the bottle does not turn blue-black in colour, indicating that food is not made in that part of the leaf. This is because the caustic soda solution inside the flask absorbs the carbon dioxide present in the air. What does this experiment show?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.586,
            0.882,
            0.624
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This experiment shows that carbon dioxide present in the air is essential for plants to prepare starch."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.625,
            0.882,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Based on Activities 10.3 and 10.4, what do you "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "conclude",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Which part of the plant is involved in the synthesis of starch?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.664,
            0.883,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Based on our learnings so far, we have found that "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "sunlight, water",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "chlorophyll",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "carbon dioxide",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are essential for the synthesis of food in plants. This process by which plants prepare food in the presence of sunlight and chlorophyll is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "photosynthesis.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A leaf is the primary site for photosynthesis. Do other green parts of the plant also perform photosynthesis? Yes, other parts of the plants which have chlorophyll also perform photosynthesis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.814,
            0.884,
            0.891
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So far, we have learnt that plants take in carbon dioxide from the air and water, and use sunlight to prepare their food by the process of photosynthesis. But have you ever thought about what more happens during this process? Do plants only take in"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
